Question first. Paragraph 1 asks whether rings of identical cardiac cells near a Hopf point carry stable rotating waves, says why a proof matters (a second Floquet multiplier tends to 1 there, so a floating-point stability verdict is fragile) and names the model. Paragraph 2 gives the results in the order of Theorems A, B and C: the cell by two proofs sharing no library and the exact link between them; the rings N = 8, 16, 32, 64 with existence, local uniqueness, non-synchrony, the period enclosure and stability with its bound and delta; then every N >= 8 and the cable, with convergence. Paragraph 3 gives the method (radii polynomial in weighted l^1; one Hill operator; Riesz-projection homotopy; the epsilon = 1/N^2 interval cover in 73 pieces) and what is not claimed (stability only where it was certified; the Hopf identification is numerical). No file was edited.

Word count: 325 by the counter that gives 523 for the current abstract (regex \w+ on the LaTeX source), or 304 whitespace-separated. Paragraphs are 65, 145 and 115 by that counter (56, 139 and 109 whitespace). The LaTeX is 1893 characters, which fits arXiv's 1920 limit; the plain text is 1807. The LaTeX is the abstract body only, to go between \begin{abstract} and \medskip; the Keywords and MSC lines stay as they are. It compiled cleanly with pdflatex in the scratchpad using the paper's \re macro. It has no em dashes and no non-ASCII characters.

